    "details": "The OpenVoxProject reports:\n\n> Trapperkeeper is the framework we use for running our Clojure based\n> applications and `trapperkeeper-authorization` is the shared\n> authentication library that parses OpenVox's `auth.conf` file and\n> such. This file allows authentication rules to be specified in\n> multiple ways, including plain strings (`foo.example.com`), wildcards\n> (`*.example.com`), or regular expressions (`/lab-\\d{3}.example.com/`).\n> Plain strings and wildcard matches were handled by the same logic and\n> this resulted in wildcard behavior incorrectly showing up in all\n> non-regex rules. The end result of this meant that a certificate name\n> of `evil.target.example.com` would be allowed to impersonate\n> `target.example.com` and access information or perform actions as the\n> target certname. In some non-default configurations, this could even\n> inject exported resources into arbitrary agent catalogs across the\n> infrastructure.\n",
    "id": "FreeBSD-2026-0598",
